# The incidence rank and cycle space of every graph this model composes that has an edge. One row
# per graph: nodes, edges, components, and the two dimensions that follow. The claimant graph has
# no edges, because delegation is filed nowhere, so it has no row.
#
# The parameter the graphs differ on
#   `rank = n - c` and `cycle space = m - n + c`, which is the parameter the graphs differ on. A
#   graph with cycles has no ordinal rank and always has a matrix rank, and confusing the two
#   produces a wrong claim: that a cyclic graph has no rank at all. It has one.
#
# Which cycle, and the implication runs one way
#   `m - n + c` counts UNDIRECTED cycles, so a zero here says the graph is a forest.
#   Well-foundedness is the DIRECTED question, whether anything descends forever, and every
#   acyclic digraph has it. A forest is well-founded; the converse fails. A diamond is four edges
#   over four nodes in one component, so its cycle space is one, and nothing in it descends
#   forever. So a zero cycle space hands you the ordinal rank and the ordinal rank hands back
#   nothing. The layer graph is both at once, and that is `checks/jagged_layer` holding it there
#   rather than an identity: a diamond in it is a layer composed twice.
#
# Where a weighted graph's rule lives
#   The cycle space is where a weighted graph's rule lives. An edge vector decomposes into the
#   column space, a gradient, plus the left nullspace, the cycle part. `B` is `m x n`, one row per
#   edge, and every one of those names depends on that: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry
#   `incidence_subspaces`, is where the orientation is declared. The cycle component of
#   `log phi` vanishing says `log phi` is a gradient, which says a potential exists: an absolute
#   log-size per unit whose differences are the filed factors. A rule written as a walk round a
#   loop is a subspace condition. `checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close` asks its interval form:
#   with each factor a range, that 1 lie inside the product round the loop, since exact closure at
#   the mode is out of reach, as that rule's header shows. Gilbert Strang's graph chapter is the
#   reference.
#
# And the cut space is where this model's OTHER rule lives
#   The two subspaces are orthogonal complements, and this model has one rule in each. A sum round a
#   loop is the cycle condition, which is the paragraph above and what
#   `checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close` asks of the unit graph. A balance AT A NODE is the cut
#   condition: what a composed layer files equals the sum over its converted parts less what was
#   eliminated, with the elimination as the source term. That is the fusion rule, it lives in the
#   column space, and `checks/jagged_layer` accuses on that side. So a filing can satisfy one and
#   break the other, and the two graphs need two rules rather than one.
#   Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entries `incidence_subspaces` and `fusion_adds_no_cycle`.
#
# A zero cycle space leaves no room for a circulation
#   Where `m - n + c` is zero the cut space is the whole edge space, so every edge vector on that
#   graph is a potential difference and nothing goes round. That is the conserved carrier written as
#   a subspace, and it is why no spectral radius arises here: a composition has no direction to
#   circulate in. Read the dimensions off this relation rather than from a sentence.
#
# The numbers
#   Run this relation for the counts. The corpus moves and a sentence does not.
#
# Components are undirected
#   `n - c` and `m - n + c` are facts about the underlying undirected graph. Reading them off the
#   directed edges gives a different and wrong `c`.
#
# One scope of `rank/graph_measures.sqlc`
#   This relation is that one at the corpus-wide scope, not a second copy of the walk.
#   `rank/decomposition.sqlc` reads the same relation, and its `filing IS NULL` rows are exactly
#   these rows, so a fix to the walk is made once.
#
# The pin is `filing IS NULL`, and the column is projected away
#   That is the shape that usually marks a slice passing as an aggregate. It is declared here and
#   in the `--` line because it is the whole graph rather than a slice of one: the per-filing cut
#   is a different question and `rank/decomposition.sqlc` is the file that asks it.
#
# Defined, not read
#   A program reads these columns, so this statement defines its vocabulary in its own
#   WITH clause: a view keeps the rows and loses the column's origin, which is where
#   NOT NULL lives. assets/sqlc/views.sqlc says what a definition is.
#
-- rank/graph_measures.sqlc at the corpus-wide scope: the two graphs as incidence matrices.
WITH
fusions AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(composition/fusions.sqlc)
),
notations AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(composition/notations.sqlc)
),
part_references AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(composition/part_references.sqlc)
),
parts AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(composition/parts.sqlc)
),
unresolved_parts AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(composition/unresolved_parts.sqlc)
),
filed AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(eliminations/filed.sqlc)
),
searched AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(eliminations/searched.sqlc)
),
fusion_parts AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(folds/fusion_parts.sqlc)
),
summed_quantities AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(layers/summed_quantities.sqlc)
),
derived_quantities AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(composition/derived_quantities.sqlc)
),
resolved_quantities AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc)
),
slacks AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(entries/slacks.sqlc)
),
demand AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(layers/demand.sqlc)
),
layers_nameplate AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(layers/nameplate.sqlc)
),
quantities AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(layers/quantities.sqlc)
),
part_quantities AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(composition/part_quantities.sqlc)
),
conversions AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(units/conversions.sqlc)
),
graph_edges AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(rank/graph_edges.sqlc)
),
graph_measures AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(rank/graph_measures.sqlc)
)
SELECT m.graph, m.n_nodes, m.m_edges, m.c_components, m.cycle_space_dim, m.rank_of_incidence
FROM (
    :compose(rank/graph_measures.sqlc)
) m
WHERE m.filing IS NULL
